{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can state the difference between current and potential difference in your own words, explain why charge and energy conservation force the series and parallel circuit rules, and correctly solve a changed mixed circuit problem by choosing and justifying the method yourself."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "I confuse current with potential difference and cannot explain why circuit rules hold. Prepare for independent problem solving.",
          "explanation": "The reported weakness is conceptual, not procedural: current and potential difference are being conflated, which is why the circuit rules feel like arbitrary formulas. The smallest useful strategy is one integrated conceptual activity that separates the two quantities and derives the series/parallel rules from conservation, followed by one independent changed circuit problem where you must choose and justify the method. No separate relearning block, repeated practice set or second check is booked because no failure has been observed yet; further remediation is contingent on the independent problem revealing a concrete error.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 60,
            "likelyMinutes": 90,
            "highMinutes": 120
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "foundation",
              "title": "Conceptual foundation and independent application",
              "outcome": "Current and potential difference are distinguished, the circuit rules are explained from conservation, and a changed circuit problem is solved independently with a justified method choice.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 60,
                "likelyMinutes": 90,
                "highMinutes": 120
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You explain the current/potential-difference distinction and the conservation origin of the series and parallel rules without prompting, and correctly solve a changed mixed circuit problem with a stated, valid method choice."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The core conceptual fix: define current as charge flow rate and potential difference as energy per unit charge, and contrast them with a concrete example so the two are no longer conflated."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Explains why the circuit rules hold: charge conservation forces equal current in series, energy conservation forces voltage division in series and equal voltage across parallel branches."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The independent application: solve a changed mixed circuit problem, choosing and justifying the method (e.g. equivalent resistance then branch currents) without assistance."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "concept_apply",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "foundation"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Distinguish current and potential difference, derive circuit rules, and solve a changed circuit independently",
            "activityType": "Integrated conceptual explanation and independent problem solving",
            "purpose": "Repair the current/potential-difference confusion, connect the circuit rules to conservation, and verify independent method selection and solution on changed work.",
            "instructions": "Part A (about 30 minutes): Write your own definitions of current (rate of charge flow, measured in amperes) and potential difference (energy transferred per unit charge, measured in volts). Give one concrete example of each in a simple circuit and state explicitly what each quantity does and does not tell you. Then explain, using charge conservation, why the current is the same through components in series, and using energy conservation, why the total potential difference across a series loop is shared among components and why parallel branches have the same potential difference across them. Part B (about 45-60 minutes): Solve the following changed circuit problem without help. A 12 V source is connected to a 4 ohm resistor in series with a parallel combination of a 6 ohm and a 12 ohm resistor. (1) State which method you will use and why it is valid here. (2) Find the equivalent resistance of the parallel pair, then the total equivalent resistance. (3) Find the total current from the source. (4) Find the potential difference across the parallel pair and the current through each parallel resistor. (5) Check your answers: verify that the branch currents sum to the total current and that the energy per unit charge accounted for across the series and parallel parts equals the source potential difference. Show all working and keep your method justification with the solution.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written correct definitions distinguishing current from potential difference, correctly explained the series and parallel rules from conservation, and correctly solved all five parts of the changed circuit problem with a stated valid method choice and a completed check; any errors are corrected with the actual corrected values shown.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 60,
              "likelyMinutes": 90,
              "highMinutes": 120
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 120,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 30,
                "completionCriterion": "Definitions of current and potential difference are written with a concrete contrast, and the series and parallel rules are explained from charge and energy conservation."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "localId": "remediation",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "foundation"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3"
            ],
            "title": "Targeted remediation on the specific error found in the independent circuit problem",
            "activityType": "Failure-triggered targeted remediation",
            "purpose": "Address only the concrete conceptual or procedural error revealed by the independent problem, rather than repeating already verified work.",
            "instructions": "Review the specific step or concept that failed in the independent circuit problem. Re-explain that step in your own words, rework only the affected part with corrected reasoning, and solve one new changed variant of that part to confirm the fix. Do not redo parts that were already correct.",
            "completionCriterion": "The specific failed step is correctly re-explained and the new changed variant of that step is solved correctly without assistance.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 30,
              "highMinutes": 45
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 45,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "CONTINGENT",
              "trigger": {
                "checkpointActivityId": "concept_apply",
                "condition": "The independent circuit problem in concept_apply contains a concrete error in distinguishing current from potential difference, in explaining a circuit rule, or in the method selection or solution."
              }
            },
            "hardDependencies": [
              "concept_apply"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has basic familiarity with Ohm's law and simple series and parallel circuits, since the reported weakness is conceptual confusion rather than complete unfamiliarity."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "No separate relearning block or repeated practice set is booked because the integrated activity already covers explanation, supported application and an independent changed example; adding more would duplicate evidence without a distinct goal.",
            "Further remediation is left contingent rather than committed so that already verified work is not repeated unless a concrete error is observed."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The exact nature of the current/potential-difference confusion is not specified, so the integrated activity is designed to surface it through the independent problem rather than assume a particular error pattern."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the integrated activity's completion criterion is met: correct definitions, correct conservation-based explanation of the circuit rules, and a correct independent solution to the changed circuit problem with a justified method choice.",
            "If the independent problem reveals a concrete error, complete the contingent remediation and stop once that specific step is corrected and confirmed on a new variant."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The plan commits one integrated 120-minute activity that combines conceptual explanation, conservation-based derivation of the circuit rules, and an independent changed circuit problem, because the reported weakness is a single conceptual confusion that does not warrant separate relearning, repeated practice and a second check. A contingent remediation activity is available only if the independent problem reveals a concrete error, so no additional work is booked unless the evidence justifies it. This keeps the stopping point bounded and avoids duplicating already verified work."
}

