            "purpose": "Address the base-rate/sensitivity confusion directly by explaining the distinction during work on a worked example, then having the learner solve and check a changed example independently.",
            "instructions": "1) Write Bayes' rule in your own words and label each term: prior (base rate), likelihood (sensitivity), false-positive rate, and posterior. 2) Work through a medical-test example: state the base rate and sensitivity explicitly, then compute the posterior probability step by step. 3) Check your arithmetic and confirm that you used the base rate as the prior and the sensitivity as the true-positive rate, not swapped. 4) Now solve a changed medical-test example with different numbers: identify the base rate and sensitivity, compute the posterior, and check your answer. 5) In one or two sentences, explain why a low base rate can make a positive test result still imply a low probability of disease even when sensitivity is high.",
